.TH SAY 1
.SH NAME
say \- speak, through ml(3)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B say
[
.B -v
.I voice
]
[
.B -o
.I file
]
[
.B -i
]
.I id ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Say
runs a voice model and writes the result to
.B /dev/audio
\(em or to
.I file
with
.BR -o ,
as signed 16-bit little-endian mono.
.B -i
first prints what the model declares.
.PP
The voice is a VITS model: phoneme numbers in, a waveform out, with no vocoder
or formant synthesis in between.
That is the reason for putting it behind
.IR ml (3)
rather than building a synthesizer \(em the interesting part is a trained
network, and the device already runs those.
.SH "IT TAKES PHONEMES, NOT TEXT"
Turning text into phonemes is a different job with a different failure mode,
which is English spelling, and doing it badly here would make a working
synthesizer look broken.
A front end belongs on top of this, not inside it.
.PP
The ids are the voice's own, from the
.B phoneme_id_map
in its
.B .onnx.json
file.
This model wants them interleaved with the pad symbol and wrapped in begin and
end:
.IP
.EX
say 1 0 20 0 59 0 24 0 120 0 27 0 100 0 2
say: 11008 samples, .688 seconds at 16000 Hz
.EE
.PP
which is
.BR hello .
.SH "IT NEEDS A BIGGER POOL THAN THE DEFAULT"
A voice is tens of megabytes and
.IR emu (1)'s
main pool is 32MB, so the model does not fit and the write fails with
.BR "out of memory: kernel" .
.IP
.EX
emu -pmain=512m -pheap=512m ...
.EE
.SH "WHAT WAS CHECKED"
Not by listening, which cannot be automated and does not regress.
The output of
.B hello
is 0.69 seconds, which is right for the word; its peak is 20187 of 32767, so it
is neither silent nor clipped; six of its seventeen frames are near-silent,
which is the lead-in and lead-out of a short utterance; its envelope varies by
a factor of five thousand within the utterance, where noise would be flat; and
it crosses zero 3033 times a second, which is the voiced-speech range rather
than a tone or a hiss.
.PP
Saying the word twice gives 0.94 seconds rather than 0.69 \(em longer, and not
double, because the model's duration predictor treats the phrase as a unit.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/say.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ml (3),
.IR audio (3)
.SH BUGS
.B "One utterance per emu process."
The second faults inside the host's neural library.
.I Emu
aliases the libc allocators into its own pool and
.I onnxruntime
uses several the aliasing does not cover \(em
.BR posix_memalign ,
.BR aligned_alloc ,
the
.B malloc_zone
calls \(em so it allocates on one heap and frees on the other.
It is not memory exhaustion and not threading; both were tried.
See
.B doc/hpc-plan.md
for the diagnosis and what fixing it would take.
.PP
This is why
.B /lib/demo/tour.sh
does not yet run: it speaks five times.
.PP
One voice, named in the source as a default.
.PP
It holds the whole waveform before writing any of it, so a long utterance is a
long silence and then all of it at once.
A model that can be run in pieces would stream; this one is asked for a phrase
and answers with a phrase.
.PP
Nothing checks that the ids belong to the voice.
An id past the end of its table is the voice's problem, and what it does about
it is not defined here.
.PP
The scales \(em how noisy, how fast, how variable \(em are fixed at what the
voice's own configuration recommends.
